Package: SAEforestLearner
Title: Scalable Mixed Effects Models with Random Forests and Other Learners for Small Area Estimation
Version: 2.0.0
Authors@R: c(person("Patrick", "Krennmair", email = "patrick.krennmair@fu-berlin.de", role = c("aut", "cph"),
                  comment = "author of the original 'SAEforest' package"),
           person("Nicolas", "Frink", email = "nicolas.frink@fu-berlin.de", role = c("aut", "cph"),
                  comment = "author of the original 'SAEforest' package"),
           person("Elia", "Vangi", email = "elia.vangi@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre"),
                  comment = "scalable engine, raster populations, aggregation, Box-Cox transformation"))
Description: Mixed Effects Random Forests (MERFs) are a data-driven,
    nonparametric alternative to current methods of Small Area Estimation
    (SAE). This is a scalable fork of 'SAEforest' 1.0.1: domain means (and
    totals) with their nonparametric bootstrap MSE are computed without ever
    holding population x bootstrap matrices in memory, the population can be
    a (very large) raster streamed from a disk cache, and random forest
    predictions use a fast multi-threaded C++ traversal of 'ranger' forests.
    Optional extensions: a Box-Cox transformed MERF with empirical-best-type
    back-transformation of domain means, heteroscedastic bootstrap variants,
    and exact bootstrap MSEs of any aggregation of domains.
    From a methodological perspective, the package builds on approaches discussed in
    Krennmair and Schmid (2022) <arXiv:2201.10933v2> and Krennmair
    et al. (2022) <arXiv:2204.10736>.
